  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 223 PM CDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nAn upper level trough is currently over the Central Great Basin and \nSouthwest US with upper ridging over the Middle and Lower \nMississippi Valley and into the Ohio and Tennessee Valley. An upper \nlow is digging off the coast of the Pacific Northwest. At the \nsurface, a front extends from the Great Lakes through central Iowa \nand into central Kansas. Scattered showers are ongoing in western \nKansas in an area of 850-700mb WAA. This activity will continue to \nmove north-northeast late this afternoon through tonight, affecting \ncentral KS. Precipitable water values in this airmass are up to \n1.25\", leading to effective rain showers. Thunderstorms look to be \nunlikely as guidance suggests instability values generally under 150 \nJ/kg, though a few claps of thunder cannot be ruled out. Minimal \nimpacts are expected with up to 0.5 inches possible mainly in \nRussell and Barton counties.\n\nBy Monday morning, the upper trough over the Central Great Basin \nwill propagate into the Central Rockies and then into the Central \nPlains by late Monday afternoon. This will keep rain chances \npossible in central KS from Monday morning through the afternoon \nwith chances increasing in south-central KS Monday night into \nTuesday morning especially as another weak shortwave trough lifts \nnorth from Texas. Severe weather is not anticipated with any of this \nactivity given the lack of instability. For temperatures, highs will \ncontinue to be above normal with readings in the lower 70s in \ncentral KS to the upper 70s in southeast KS on Monday afternoon. For \nTuesday afternoon, highs in the lower 70s are expected across the \narea.\n\nBy mid week, well above normal temperatures will return with highs \nin the upper 70s to near 80 from Wednesday to Friday. Lows are \nforecast to be in the upper 50s to lower 60s on Wednesday and \nThursday night. Additional rain chances may be possible on Wednesday \nnight and Thursday night as 700mb WAA persists across the area. Rain \nchances will increase areawide on Friday and into the weekend as \nanother upper wave slides across the region. Stay tuned as we \ncontinue to refine this forecast.",
